(This is a large \(1.53m x 0.66m x 0.59m\) hollow thin-wall shell with a very low fill ratio \(0.0178\), consistent with an automotive fuel tank.) Tj
T* (Wall thickness is thin and fairly uniform \(mean 5.04mm\) except at pinch-weld/boss zones which read up to 18.34mm — a classic EBM) Tj
T* (parison pinch-off signature, not a cast or machined solid. The user has forced blow_moulding as the process; given the part's role \(fuel) Tj
T* (tank\), size, and thick weld zones, Extrusion Blow Moulding \(EBM\) with a single large clam-shell mould is the correct subtype \(vs IBM for) Tj
T* (small precision bottles or SBM for PET beverage containers\). Material was undecided in the geometry package; HDPE with a 6-layer) Tj
T* (HDPE/EVOH barrier structure was selected as the industry-standard resin for automotive fuel tanks \(chemical resistance + permeation) Tj
T* (control\), over PP \(used for drums/IBM bottles\) and PET \(used for SBM beverage bottles, unsuitable for hydrocarbon service\). Net part) Tj
T* (weight was recalculated at HDPE's true density \(0.955 g/cm3\) rather than the generic 1.05 g/cm3 'Plastic' figure supplied, giving ~10.12kg) Tj
T* (net, with an estimated ~2.5kg of trim flash from the continuous parison process \(pinch weld waste, typically reground and reused\). Mould) Tj
T* (cost, cavity count, and cycle time \(blow/cool/open-close\) are rule-of-thumb estimates informed by geometry \(size, wall, undercuts\) since) Tj
T* (no OCCT-deterministic blow-moulding parameters were provided beyond wall thickness.) Tj
